/* Hinglish for Capstone: discover, build, break, measure, ship and defend a system (chapter:ch21cap), in simple words.
   Written by tools/sheet.mjs apply; keyed on the English line. Loads last, so it wins. */
Object.assign(window.HING = window.HING || {}, {
  'Do not start with slides. Your problem is Bharat Privacy Guard. Gather what you already have: the repository (an online folder that remembers every change), the issue list and the evaluation dataset (your questions with right answers). Then build the smallest slice that works from start to finish, like one thin cut through a whole cake.':
    'Slides se shuru mat kijiye. Aapki problem Bharat Privacy Guard hai. Jo aapke paas pehle se hai woh ikattha kijiye: repository (ek online folder jo har badlav yaad rakhta hai), issue list aur evaluation dataset (sawaal aur unke sahi jawaab). Phir sabse chhota slice banaiye jo shuru se aakhir tak chalta ho, jaise poore cake ka ek patla tukda.',
  'Grow Bharat Privacy Guard into one finished product. Give its rule-keeper two forms: a fixed workflow (a set recipe) and a helper that can use tools but has a step limit. A person approves risky actions. Add structured outputs (a form with fixed boxes), one input that is not text, an evaluation harness (the test rig), tracing and cost tracking. Add security controls. Then test it with real users and improve it.':
    'Bharat Privacy Guard ko ek poora product banaiye. Uske rule-keeper ko do roop dijiye: fixed workflow (tai recipe) aur ek helper jo tools use kare par step limit ke saath. Risky kaam ko ek insaan approve kare. Structured outputs (fixed khaanon wala form), ek aisa input jo text nahi hai, evaluation harness (test ka dhancha), tracing aur cost tracking jodiye. Security controls jodiye. Phir real users se test kijiye aur behtar banaiye.',
  'Break it on purpose in at least ten kinds of failure: a question with no answer, a poisoned document, prompt injection (a hidden note that says "ignore your teacher"), an unauthorised tool request, a malformed tool call, a stale permission, a multilingual query, a poor scan or audio, a model swap, and a rise in traffic or cost.':
    'Kam se kam das tarah ki failure jaan-boojhkar kijiye: aisa sawaal jiska jawaab nahi hai, poisoned document, prompt injection (chhupi hui parchi jo kehti hai "teacher ki baat mat suno"), bina ijazat ka tool request, bigdi hui tool call, purani permission, kai bhasha wala query, kharab scan ya audio, model badalna, aur traffic ya cost ka achanak badhna.',
  'The running system, a live URL with real users, and an evidence pack (a folder of proof). The pack holds the PRD (the recipe card for the product), the architecture, the threat model, the evaluation plan, the dashboard, the unit economics, the decision log, the rollout and rollback plan, the vendor scorecard and an explanation for executives.':
    'Chalta hua system, real users wala live URL, aur ek evidence pack (saboot ka folder). Pack mein hain: PRD (product ka recipe card), architecture, threat model, evaluation plan, dashboard, unit economics, decision log, rollout aur rollback plan, vendor scorecard aur executives ke liye ek samjhaane wala note.',
  'Show three failures you found yourself, three improvements with numbers, evidence from real users, unit economics that hold up (the money maths per task), the risks that remain, and what you chose not to solve.':
    'Teen failures dikhaiye jo aapne khud dhoondhe, teen improvements numbers ke saath, real users ka evidence, unit economics jo tikte hain (har task ka paise ka hisaab), bache hue risks, aur jo aapne solve na karne ka faisla kiya.',
  'Findings document':
    'Findings document',
  'A plain, true record, like a repair report: what broke, the evidence, the root cause (the real reason), the fix, and what you chose not to solve. It is the opposite of a brochure.':
    'Ek saada, sachcha record, jaise repair report: kya toota, evidence, root cause (asli wajah), fix, aur aapne kya solve nahi kiya. Yeh brochure ka ulta hai.',
  'Failure class':
    'Failure class',
  'A kind of failure, not one single example. Think of a pile in your complaint sorting. Ten classes make ten tests that mean something, instead of ten stories.':
    'Failure ki ek tarah, koi ek example nahi. Socho jaise shikayaton ki ek dher. Das classes ka matlab das aise test jinka matlab hai, das kahaniyan nahi.',
  'The risk that is left after a control (a safety step) is in place. You say it out loud instead of leaving people to guess.':
    'Woh risk jo control (suraksha ka kadam) lagne ke baad bhi bacha rehta hai. Aap use khul kar batate hain, logon ko andaza lagane nahi dete.',
  'Evidence pack':
    'Evidence pack',
  'A folder of proof that lets somebody else check your claims, like showing your working in a maths exam, instead of just asking to be trusted.':
    'Saboot ka folder jisse koi aur aapke daavon ko jaanch sake, jaise maths exam mein apna working dikhana, bas bharosa maangna nahi.',
  'Kill criteria':
    'Kill criteria',
  'The stop sign you write down before you need it. It tells you when to stop. Without it, you keep working on a problem the evidence says will not work.':
    'Rukne ka board jo aap zaroorat se pehle likh dete hain. Yeh batata hai kab rukna hai. Iske bina aap us problem par lage rehte hain jise evidence kehta hai ki kaam nahi karegi.',
  'This is one capstone (the big final project), not two. Take a real problem with users you can reach. Carry it from the first talk with users, through a live launch, to a defence in front of executives and engineers. You build one applied AI system, break it on purpose, and measure what you fixed. Then you show it works as a product, not only as a demo.':
    'Yeh ek capstone (aakhri bada project) hai, do nahi. Ek real problem lijiye jiske users tak aap pahunch sakte hain. Use users se pehli baat se le kar live launch tak, aur phir executives aur engineers ke saamne defence tak le jaiye. Aap ek applied AI system banate hain, use jaan-boojhkar todte hain, aur jo fix kiya use measure karte hain. Phir dikhate hain ki woh product ki tarah chalta hai, sirf demo ki tarah nahi.',
  'Take a real problem, with users you can reach, from the first talk all the way to a live product.':
    'Ek real problem lijiye, jiske users tak aap pahunch sakte hain, aur pehli baat se le kar live product tak le jaiye.',
  'Explain a system you built from the ground up, without a framework (ready-made code) doing the explaining for you.':
    'Apne banaye system ko bilkul buniyaad se samjhaiye, bina kisi framework (bana-banaya code) ke aapki jagah samjhaye.',
  'Show before-and-after numbers for the failures you found and fixed, and for the users who used it.':
    'Jo failures aapne dhoondhe aur fix kiye, unke pehle aur baad ke numbers dikhaiye, aur un users ke bhi jinhone use chalaya.',
  'Defend the business case as carefully as the architecture. That means unit economics (money per task), growth and vendor risk (what if your model company changes).':
    'Business case ka bachav utni hi sawdhani se kijiye jitni architecture ka. Matlab unit economics (har task ka paisa), growth aur vendor risk (agar aapki model company badal jaye toh kya).',
  'Say clearly what you chose not to solve.':
    'Saaf kahiye ki aapne kya solve na karne ka faisla kiya.',
  'Ship Bharat Privacy Guard, and write the findings document':
    'Bharat Privacy Guard ship kijiye, aur findings document likhiye',
  'This is the last capstone of the technical course. Everything you made now comes together in one repository and one document. Keep the document plain and true. A findings document that reads like a brochure is not doing its job. Describe the project as "a privacy tool that helps an organisation collect and share less personal data". Never say it makes anyone compliant.':
    'Yeh technical course ka aakhri capstone hai. Jo kuch aapne banaya, woh ab ek repository aur ek document mein jama hota hai. Document saada aur sachcha rakhiye. Jo findings document brochure jaisa padhta hai, woh apna kaam nahi karta. Project ko aise batayiye: "a privacy tool that helps an organisation collect and share less personal data". Yeh kabhi mat kahiye ki yeh kisi ko compliant banata hai.',
  'Your repository, with the working system and its README (the first page that says how to run it), plus a written findings document. You hand in both together.':
    'Aapki repository, jismein chalta system aur uska README (pehla page jo batata hai ise kaise chalana hai) ho, aur saath mein likha hua findings document. Dono ek saath jama kijiye.',
  'Make the repository work on a clean machine. Someone else must be able to clone it (copy it down), follow the README and run the answer key (your questions with right answers).':
    'Repository ko ek saaf machine par chalaiye. Koi aur use clone kar sake (neeche copy kar sake), README follow kar sake aur answer key (sahi jawaab wale sawaal) chala sake.',
  'Add the architecture diagram (a map of the parts) and the decision log (a diary of why you chose what).':
    'Architecture diagram (hisson ka naksha) aur decision log (aapne kya aur kyun chuna, uski diary) jodiye.',
  'Add the final answer key and the scoreboard from before and after your fixes.':
    'Aakhri answer key aur apne fixes se pehle aur baad ka scoreboard jodiye.',
  'Add the results of the attacks you tried in Chapter 13, and a few sample traces (the step-by-step record of one run).':
    'Chapter 13 mein jo attacks aapne try kiye unke results jodiye, aur kuch sample traces (ek run ka step-by-step record).',
  'Add the workflow-versus-agent table from Chapter 26 for the rule-keeper. Say which one ships, and name the number that decided it.':
    'Rule-keeper ke liye Chapter 26 ki workflow-versus-agent table jodiye. Batayiye kaun sa ship hota hai, aur woh number likhiye jisne faisla kiya.',
  'Add the cost and speed at the volume you expect. Then say what changes at ten times the volume.':
    'Jitna volume aap expect karte hain, us par cost aur speed jodiye. Phir batayiye dus guna volume par kya badalta hai.',
  'Add the requirements, the risk register (a list of what could go wrong), the system card (a fact sheet about the system) and the rollout and rollback plan.':
    'Requirements, risk register (kya galat ho sakta hai uski list), system card (system ki fact sheet) aur rollout aur rollback plan jodiye.',
  'Add real-user evidence: notes from sessions with three to five people who are not your teammates on the route (five to ten in the full version). Add the one change you made because of them.':
    'Real-user evidence jodiye: teen se paanch aise logon ke saath sessions ke notes jo route par aapke teammates nahi hain (poore version mein paanch se das). Woh ek badlav bhi jodiye jo aapne unki wajah se kiya.',
  'Write the findings document. Say what you built, what broke, who used it and what the evidence shows. Include three failures you found yourself, each with evidence. Include three fixes, each with numbers from before and after.':
    'Findings document likhiye. Batayiye aapne kya banaya, kya toota, kisne use kiya aur evidence kya dikhata hai. Teen failures dijiye jo aapne khud dhoondhe, har ek evidence ke saath. Teen fixes dijiye, har ek ke pehle aur baad ke numbers ke saath.',
  'End with what is still risky and what you left out on purpose, such as other Indian languages, the gateway and the browser extension.':
    'Ant mein likhiye ki ab bhi kya risky hai aur kya aapne jaan-boojhkar chhoda, jaise doosri Indian bhashayein, gateway aur browser extension.',
  'A repository that works on a clean machine.':
    'Ek repository jo saaf machine par chalti hai.',
  'Three failures you found yourself, each with evidence.':
    'Teen failures jo aapne khud dhoondhe, har ek evidence ke saath.',
  'Three fixes, each with before and after numbers.':
    'Teen fixes, har ek pehle aur baad ke numbers ke saath.',
  'A table that compares the fixed workflow and the agent, with the number that decided which one ships.':
    'Ek table jo fixed workflow aur agent ko tolti hai, us number ke saath jisne tay kiya kaun sa ship hoga.',
  'Evidence from three to five real users on the route (five to ten in the full version), who are not your teammates.':
    'Route par teen se paanch real users ka evidence (poore version mein paanch se das), jo aapke teammates nahi hain.',
  'The cost per message (like the price of one SMS), and what happens to it at ten times the volume.':
    'Har message ka cost (jaise ek SMS ka daam), aur dus guna volume par uska kya hota hai.',
  'A five-minute talk a non-technical leader could repeat, and a twenty-minute talk that an engineer cannot easily poke holes in.':
    'Paanch minute ki baat jo koi non-technical leader dohra sake, aur bees minute ki baat jisme koi engineer aasani se chhed na kar sake.',
  'Everything in Part V':
    'Part V ki har cheez',
  'The capstone brings all of it together, like the final dish made from every lesson.':
    'Capstone sab kuch ek saath laata hai, jaise har sabak se bani aakhri dish.',
  'A release gate (a check before launch) and a risk register':
    'Ek release gate (launch se pehle ki jaanch) aur ek risk register',
  'Chapters 29 and 31 give you the evidence you will defend with.':
    'Chapter 29 aur 31 aapko woh evidence dete hain jisse aap bachav karenge.',
  'Track A\'s discovery and strategy artifacts (the papers you made there)':
    'Track A ke discovery aur strategy artifacts (wahan banaye gaye papers)',
  'They help, but you can start without them. If you have them, the discovery and design stages reuse them.':
    'Yeh madad karte hain, par inke bina bhi shuru kar sakte hain. Agar aapke paas hain, toh discovery aur design stages unhe dobara use karte hain.',
  'The full version takes about thirty hours, over four to six weeks. It brings the whole course together, like the final exam project of a school year. The technical capstone and the product, business, real-user and defence work are now one project, not two.':
    'Poora version lagbhag 30 ghante leta hai, 4 se 6 hafton mein. Yeh poore course ko ek jagah laata hai, jaise school saal ka aakhri exam project. Technical capstone aur product, business, real-user aur defence ka kaam ab ek hi project hain, do nahi.',
  'Your problem is chosen: Bharat Privacy Guard. Stages 34.1 to 34.3 are in your repository. The route gives this chapter about eight hours. Do five stages: 34.4 put the system together, 34.5 run the release gate (the check before launch), 34.6 break it three ways, 34.9 write the cost, 34.11 write the findings. Show it to three to five people if you can (34.8). The rest comes later.':
    'Aapki problem chuni hui hai: Bharat Privacy Guard. Stages 34.1 se 34.3 aapki repository mein hain, unhe ikattha kijiye. Route is chapter ko lagbhag aath ghante deta hai. Paanch stages kijiye: 34.4 system ko jodiye, 34.5 release gate chalaiye (launch se pehle ki jaanch), 34.6 use teen tarah se todiye, 34.9 cost likhiye, 34.11 findings likhiye. Ho sake toh teen se paanch logon ko dikhaiye (34.8). Baaki route ke baad aata hai.',
  'In the last chapter you saw what it takes to run an AI system for real users. Now you do the whole thing yourself. You start with the first talk with a user and end with the final defence.':
    'Pichhle chapter mein aapne dekha ki real users ke liye AI system chalane mein kya lagta hai. Ab aap poora kaam khud karte hain. Aap user se pehli baat se shuru karte hain aur aakhri defence par khatam.',
  'First, gather every artifact (every paper or file) you made in Part V. These are the harness (the test rig), the model selection card, the context budget (how much fits on the model\'s desk) and the retrieval architecture. Also the tool policy, the release gate, the dashboard, the risk register, the PRD and the decision pack. Every stage below reuses one of them.':
    'Pehle Part V mein banaya har artifact (har paper ya file) ikattha kijiye. Yeh hain harness (test ka dhancha), model selection card, context budget (model ki mez par kitna aata hai) aur retrieval architecture. Saath mein tool policy, release gate, dashboard, risk register, PRD aur decision pack. Neeche ka har stage inmein se ek ko dobara use karta hai.',
  'You may have done some of Track B\'s optional modules too. If so, reuse them. B1\'s cost model fits the measure stage. B3\'s tracking plan fits the ship stage, and so do B6\'s user sessions. B5\'s release checklist fits the observe stage. You do not need any of them to start.':
    'Ho sakta hai aapne Track B ke kuch optional modules bhi kiye hon. Agar haan, toh unhe bhi dobara use kijiye. B1 ka cost model measure stage mein fit hota hai. B3 ka tracking plan ship stage mein fit hota hai, aur B6 ke user sessions bhi. B5 ki release checklist observe stage mein fit hoti hai. Shuru karne ke liye inmein se kisi ki zaroorat nahi.',
  'What do you get at the end? A system you can explain without leaning on anyone\'s framework. Real users who have used it. And an honest page about what broke when you tested it and what it costs to run.':
    'Ant mein aapko kya milta hai? Ek system jise aap kisi ke framework par tike bina samjha sakte hain. Real users jinhone use chalaya. Aur ek imaandaar page ki test mein kya toota aur ise chalane mein kitna kharch aata hai.',
  'This matters because employers ask three things. What can you build? Did anyone want it? What goes wrong, and what do you do about it?':
    'Yeh isliye zaroori hai kyunki employers teen cheezein poochte hain. Aap kya bana sakte hain? Kisi ne ise chaha? Kya galat hota hai, aur aap uske baare mein kya karte hain?',
  'The brief':
    'The brief',
  'Your problem is Bharat Privacy Guard, and you can reach its users within two weeks. Go through eleven stages, like eleven stops on a train line: discover, define, design, build, evaluate, break, observe, ship, measure, iterate and defend.':
    'Aapki problem Bharat Privacy Guard hai, aur aap do hafte ke andar uske users tak pahunch sakte hain. Gyarah stages se guzariye, jaise train line ke gyarah station: discover, define, design, build, evaluate, break, observe, ship, measure, iterate aur defend.',
  'Build one applied AI system that joins at least four skills from Part V. A good start is retrieval that respects permissions, tool calling, one helper step that has a step limit, evaluation, tracing and security tests. Ship it to real users before you defend it. It should be one journey, like one trip from home to school, not a pile of separate demos.':
    'Ek applied AI system banaiye jo Part V ki kam se kam chaar skills ko jodta ho. Achhi shuruat hai: retrieval jo permissions ka dhyaan rakhe, tool calling, ek helper step jisme step limit ho, evaluation, tracing aur security tests. Defend karne se pehle ise real users tak ship kijiye. Yeh ek hi safar hona chahiye, jaise ghar se school ka ek raasta, alag-alag demos ka dher nahi.',
  'The eleven stages':
    'Gyarah stages',
  'The table below lists the eleven stages in order, like the stops on a route map. They are the detail inside stage 12 of the thirteen stages on the project page. For each one, the second column says what proof you must have before you move on. Read it once now, and come back to it as you work.':
    'Neeche ki table gyarah stages ko kram se dikhati hai, jaise route map ke station. Yeh project page ke terah stages mein se stage 12 ke andar ki detail hai. Har ek ke liye, doosra column batata hai aage badhne se pehle aapke paas kaun sa saboot hona chahiye. Ise abhi ek baar padhiye, aur kaam karte waqt wapas aaiye.',
  'Required evidence':
    'Zaroori evidence',
  'Five interviews, a jobs-to-be-done statement (the job the user hires the product to do), a scan of the market and competitors, and a problem brief backed by evidence.':
    'Paanch interviews, ek jobs-to-be-done statement (woh kaam jiske liye user product ko rakhta hai), market aur competitors ka scan, aur ek problem brief jo evidence se judi ho.',
  '34.2 Define':
    '34.2 Define',
  'The user, the pain, the baseline (where things stand today), the scope, the non-goals (what you will not do), the data, the tools, the risks, and a measurable acceptance criterion (a number that says it works). Together these are your capstone AI PRD.':
    'User, pain, baseline (aaj cheezein kahan khadi hain), scope, non-goals (jo aap nahi karenge), data, tools, risks, aur ek measurable acceptance criterion (ek number jo batata hai ki yeh kaam karta hai). Yeh sab milkar aapka capstone AI PRD hai.',
  'A one-page product and system design, plus the vision, strategy, roadmap (what comes first, next and later) and OKRs (goals with numbers), a stakeholder map (who cares and who can block), a pricing hypothesis (your guess at the price) and your kill criteria.':
    'Ek page ka product aur system design, saath mein vision, strategy, roadmap (kya pehle, kya baad mein) aur OKRs (numbers wale goals), stakeholder map (kaun fikr karta hai aur kaun rok sakta hai), pricing hypothesis (daam ka aapka andaza) aur aapke kill criteria.',
  '34.4 Build':
    '34.4 Build',
  'Working code, tests, versioned prompts and settings (every change saved with a number), an architecture diagram and a threat model (a list of how someone could attack it).':
    'Chalta code, tests, versioned prompts aur settings (har badlav number ke saath saved), ek architecture diagram aur ek threat model (kaise koi ispar hamla kar sakta hai, uski list).',
  '34.5 Evaluate':
    '34.5 Evaluate',
  '30 to 50 evaluation cases (questions with right answers), traces, and measurements of retrieval, answers, tools, safety, latency (waiting time) and cost.':
    '30 se 50 evaluation cases (sahi jawaab wale sawaal), traces, aur retrieval, answers, tools, safety, latency (intezaar ka samay) aur cost ki measurements.',
  '34.6 Break':
    '34.6 Break',
  'At least ten deliberate failure tests, saved before you fix anything.':
    'Kam se kam das jaan-boojhkar kiye failure tests, kuch bhi fix karne se pehle saved.',
  '34.7 Observe':
    '34.7 Observe',
  'A security go/no-go checklist (a list that says launch or hold), and a dashboard (one screen of numbers) with six panels connected to real traces.':
    'Ek security go/no-go checklist (list jo kehti hai launch ya ruko), aur ek dashboard (numbers ki ek screen) jismein chhe panels real traces se jude hon.',
  'A live URL, monitoring, 5 to 10 real users, a support log and a timed rollback test (undo a change and time it).':
    'Ek live URL, monitoring, 5 se 10 real users, ek support log aur ek timed rollback test (ek badlav wapas lijiye aur samay naapiye).',
  '34.9 Measure':
    '34.9 Measure',
  'Before-and-after numbers for your optimisations, ADRs (short notes on why you chose something), and a unit-economics model at base usage and at high usage.':
    'Aapke optimisations ke pehle aur baad ke numbers, ADRs (kisi cheez ko chunne ki wajah ke chhote notes), aur base usage aur high usage par ek unit-economics model.',
  '34.10 Iterate':
    '34.10 Iterate',
  'One improvement based on evidence, before-and-after product and AI metrics (numbers you watch), and an entry in the decision log.':
    'Evidence par based ek improvement, pehle aur baad ke product aur AI metrics (woh numbers jo aap dekhte hain), aur decision log mein ek entry.',
  '34.11 Defend':
    '34.11 Defend',
  'A ten-slide deck for executives, a twenty-minute technical defence and a public case study (a short story of your project).':
    'Executives ke liye das slides ka deck, bees minute ka technical defence aur ek public case study (aapke project ki chhoti kahani).',
  'A portfolio project is believable when you can explain its failures, show that someone wanted it, and say what it costs to run. Anyone can show a system that works on the demo path, the way a shop only shows its best shelf.':
    'Portfolio project tab bharose ke laayak hota hai jab aap uski failures samjha sakein, dikha sakein ki kisi ne ise chaha, aur bata sakein ki ise chalane mein kitna kharch aata hai. Demo ke raaste par chalne wala system koi bhi dikha sakta hai, jaise dukaan sirf apni sabse achhi shelf dikhati hai.',
  'Now the units. Each one covers a single stage, with the same rows every time: the goal, the idea, what to prove, what to build and what to break. Then come what to hand in, a question to check yourself, and why it matters. Do them in order, because each one feeds the next, like links in a chain.':
    'Ab units. Har unit ek stage ko cover karta hai, har baar wahi rows: goal, idea, kya prove karna hai, kya banana hai aur kya todna hai. Phir kya jama karna hai, khud ko jaanchne ka ek sawaal, aur yeh kyun zaroori hai. Inhe kram se kijiye, kyunki har ek agle ko khilata hai, zanjeer ki kadiyon ki tarah.',
  'Discover: a real problem with reachable users':
    'Discover: ek real problem, jiske users tak aap pahunch sakein',
  'Find a problem with a group of users you can actually reach. Write a problem brief (a short paper about the problem) backed by evidence.':
    'Ek aisi problem dhoondhiye jiske users ke group tak aap sach mein pahunch sakte hain. Evidence se judi ek problem brief (problem par chhota paper) likhiye.',
  'The best capstones come from a job you have done, a team you know or a community you belong to. Look for a problem that comes up often, costs real time or money, and is solved badly today.':
    'Sabse achhe capstones us kaam se aate hain jo aapne kiya hai, us team se jise aap jaante hain ya us samudaay se jisme aap hain. Aisi problem dhoondhiye jo baar-baar aaye, asli samay ya paisa khaye, aur aaj kharab tareeke se solve hoti ho.',
  'Score your problem on four tests. Can you reach users? That means: can you book five interviews this week? Does it happen often? Is it costly? And does it need AI, not just a form or a formula?':
    'Apni problem ko chaar tests par number dijiye. Kya aap users tak pahunch sakte hain? Matlab: kya aap isi hafte paanch interviews book kar sakte hain? Kya yeh aksar hoti hai? Kya yeh mehngi padti hai? Aur kya ismein AI chahiye, sirf ek form ya formula nahi?',
  'Run five interviews. Write a jobs-to-be-done statement and a journey map (the user\'s trip, step by step), and scan the market and the competitors.':
    'Paanch interviews kijiye. Ek jobs-to-be-done statement aur ek journey map (user ka safar, kadam-dar-kadam) likhiye, aur market aur competitors ka scan kijiye.',
  'Challenge your idea with the question "why not ordinary software that always gives the same answer?" If a form would solve the problem, it is not a capstone.':
    'Apne idea ko is sawaal se chunauti dijiye: "why not ordinary software that always gives the same answer?" Agar ek form se problem solve ho jaye, toh yeh capstone nahi hai.',
  'A problem brief backed by evidence, with a jobs-to-be-done statement.':
    'Evidence se judi ek problem brief, jobs-to-be-done statement ke saath.',
  'Can you trace each claim in your problem brief back to an interview note? Can you name one assumption (a guess you believed) that the evidence changed?':
    'Kya aap problem brief ke har daave ko ek interview note tak wapas le ja sakte hain? Kya aap ek assumption (ek maani hui baat) bata sakte hain jise evidence ne badal diya?',
  'If nothing you believed changed after five interviews, you may have heard only what you expected to hear.':
    'Agar paanch interviews ke baad aapki koi bhi maanyata nahi badli, toh shayad aapne sirf wahi suna jo aap sunna chahte the.',
  'Define the problem and PRD':
    'Define the problem and PRD',
  'Turn the problem into a system with acceptance criteria (numbers that say it works) you can measure.':
    'Problem ko ek system mein badliye, aise acceptance criteria (numbers jo batate hain ki yeh kaam karta hai) ke saath jinhe aap measure kar sakein.',
  'A capstone becomes believable when the problem is clear and the cost of failure is clear too.':
    'Capstone tab bharose ke laayak banta hai jab problem saaf ho aur failure ki keemat bhi saaf ho.',
  'Write the problem for Bharat Privacy Guard: who the user is, what goes wrong for them today, and the number that would show it is fixed.':
    'Bharat Privacy Guard ki problem likhiye: user kaun hai, aaj unke saath kya galat hota hai, aur woh number jo dikhayega ki yeh theek ho gaya.',
  'Write down the user, the pain, the baseline (where things stand today), the scope, the non-goals (what you will not do), the data, the tools, the risks and the metrics (numbers you watch).':
    'User, pain, baseline (aaj cheezein kahan hain), scope, non-goals (jo aap nahi karenge), data, tools, risks aur metrics (jo numbers aap dekhte hain) likh daliye.',
  'Ask "why not ordinary software that always gives the same answer?" again. This time, ask it about your actual scope, not the general case.':
    '"Why not ordinary software that always gives the same answer?" phir poochiye. Is baar apne asli scope ke baare mein poochiye, aam case ke baare mein nahi.',
  'The capstone AI PRD (the recipe card for your product).':
    'Capstone AI PRD (aapke product ka recipe card).',
  'Can you say the business outcome (what the business gains) in one sentence?':
    'Kya aap business outcome (business ko kya milta hai) ek vaakya mein keh sakte hain?',
  'This is the anchor (the heavy weight that holds a boat) for the product. Everything below hangs from it.':
    'Yeh product ka anchor hai (naav ko thaamne wala bhaari lanagar). Neeche ki har cheez isi se judi hai.',
  'Design the product and the system together':
    'Design the product and the system together',
  'Put the product decision and the technical decision on one page, because each one limits the other.':
    'Product ka faisla aur technical faisla ek page par rakhiye, kyunki ek doosre ko seemit karta hai.',
  'Whether a user needs an answer in two seconds or by Monday decides the architecture. And whether the architecture can hit a target decides what you can promise.':
    'User ko jawaab do second mein chahiye ya Monday tak, yeh architecture tay karta hai. Aur architecture target tak pahunch sakta hai ya nahi, yeh tay karta hai ki aap kya vaada kar sakte hain.',
  'Fill in a design with nine lines: the user and the job, the activation moment (when the user first gets value), the AI approach, why not an agent, the evaluation, the boundaries, the cost, and the North Star (the one number that guides you).':
    'Nau lines ka ek design bhariye: user aur kaam, activation moment (jab user ko pehli baar fayda milta hai), AI approach, agent kyun nahi, evaluation, boundaries, cost, aur North Star (woh ek number jo raasta dikhata hai).',
  'Add the vision, the strategy, the roadmap, the OKRs, a stakeholder map, a pricing hypothesis, and your kill criteria, which is the stop sign that tells you when to stop.':
    'Vision, strategy, roadmap, OKRs, stakeholder map, pricing hypothesis, aur apne kill criteria jodiye, jo rukne ka board hai jo batata hai kab rukna hai.',
  'Find one line in your design that you chose because it was interesting, not because it was needed, and cut it.':
    'Apne design mein ek line dhoondhiye jo aapne isliye chuni kyunki woh dilchasp thi, isliye nahi ki zaroori thi, aur use kaat dijiye.',
  'A one-page product and system design, plus a stakeholder map.':
    'Ek page ka product aur system design, saath mein ek stakeholder map.',
  'Does every technical choice on the page link to a product reason? Does every product choice link to something the system can really do?':
    'Kya page par har technical choice ek product wajah se judi hai? Kya har product choice kisi aisi cheez se judi hai jo system sach mein kar sakta hai?',
  'Kill criteria written now, before you get attached to the idea, are the ones you will really honour.':
    'Kill criteria jo abhi likhe jaate hain, idea se lagaav hone se pehle, wahi hain jinhe aap sach mein maanenge.',
  'Build the vertical slice':
    'Build the vertical slice',
  'Build the smallest system that works from start to finish, before you add anything clever.':
    'Sabse chhota system banaiye jo shuru se aakhir tak chalta ho, kuch bhi chalaak jodne se pehle.',
  'A vertical slice (one thin cut through every layer, like a slice of layered cake) proves the architecture holds from end to end. A threat model added afterwards is an excuse, not a control.':
    'Vertical slice (har layer ke beech se ek patla kata hua tukda, jaise layer wale cake ka slice) saabit karta hai ki architecture shuru se ant tak tikta hai. Baad mein jodha gaya threat model ek bahana hai, control nahi.',
  'Build the path from input, to retrieval and context, to the model, to a structured answer, to a citation (a note saying where the answer came from). Write the threat model alongside the first version, not after it.':
    'Raasta banaiye: input se, retrieval aur context tak, phir model tak, phir structured answer tak, phir citation tak (ek note ki jawaab kahan se aaya). Threat model pehle version ke saath likhiye, uske baad nahi.',
  'Add a tool only when you need one. Keep the repository easy to copy and run, with a README, a requirements file (a list of what to install) and a command that runs it the same way every time.':
    'Tool tabhi jodiye jab zaroorat ho. Repository ko copy karke chalana aasaan rakhiye: ek README, ek requirements file (kya install karna hai uski list) aur ek command jo ise har baar ek hi tareeke se chalaye.',
  'Ask a question that has no answer, and record how the system fails. This is your baseline (where you start).':
    'Aisa sawaal poochiye jiska jawaab nahi hai, aur likhiye ki system kaise fail hota hai. Yeh aapka baseline hai (jahan se aap shuru karte hain).',
  'A running repository, architecture v1 and a threat model.':
    'Ek chalti repository, architecture v1 aur ek threat model.',
  'Can you run the system from a clean environment? Could someone else repeat your experiment without asking what you did by hand?':
    'Kya aap system ko saaf environment se chala sakte hain? Kya koi aur aapka experiment dohra sakta hai bina poochhe ki aapne haath se kya kiya?',
  'A small system that works beats a large diagram, like a small shop that is open beats a big plan for a shop.':
    'Chhota system jo chalta hai, bade diagram se behtar hai, jaise khuli hui chhoti dukaan dukaan ke bade plan se behtar hai.',
  'Add evaluation and observability':
    'Add evaluation and observability',
  'Turn the capstone into something you can measure, like a child\'s height marked on the wall.':
    'Capstone ko aisi cheez banaiye jise aap naap sakein, jaise deewar par nishaan lagi bachche ki height.',
  'Every change should leave evidence behind, like footprints.':
    'Har badlav apne peeche evidence chhode, jaise kadmon ke nishaan.',
  'Create 30 to 50 evaluation cases (questions with right answers) and collect traces.':
    '30 se 50 evaluation cases (sahi jawaab wale sawaal) banaiye aur traces ikattha kijiye.',
  'Add measurements for retrieval, answers, tools, safety, latency (waiting time) and cost.':
    'Retrieval, answers, tools, safety, latency (intezaar ka samay) aur cost ke liye measurements jodiye.',
  'Change the model, the prompt or the index, and reproduce the drop in quality.':
    'Model, prompt ya index badliye, aur quality mein aayi giraawat ko dobara dikhaiye.',
  'A golden dataset (questions with trusted right answers), an evaluation harness and an observability report.':
    'Ek golden dataset (bharose ke sahi jawaab wale sawaal), ek evaluation harness aur ek observability report.',
  'Can you explain why the latest version is better?':
    'Kya aap samjha sakte hain ki sabse naya version behtar kyun hai?',
  'This is where the project turns into engineering evidence.':
    'Yahan project engineering evidence ban jata hai.',
  'Break it deliberately':
    'Break it deliberately',
  'Reproduce at least ten failure classes (ten kinds of failure).':
    'Kam se kam das failure classes (das tarah ki failure) dobara hoti dikhaiye.',
  'A portfolio project is more believable when you can explain what broke.':
    'Portfolio project zyada bharose ke laayak lagta hai jab aap samjha sakein ki kya toota.',
  'Test each of these. A question with no answer. A poisoned document. Prompt injection (a hidden note that says "ignore your teacher"). An unauthorised tool. A malformed tool call. A stale permission. Multilingual input. A poor scan or audio. A model swap. A rise in traffic or cost.':
    'In sabko test kijiye. Aisa sawaal jiska jawaab nahi. Ek poisoned document. Prompt injection (chhupi hui parchi jo kehti hai "teacher ki baat mat suno"). Bina ijazat ka tool. Bigdi hui tool call. Purani permission. Kai bhasha wala input. Kharab scan ya audio. Model badalna. Traffic ya cost ka badhna.',
  'For each failure, capture the evidence, find the root cause (the real reason) and note the control (what stops it next time).':
    'Har failure ke liye evidence pakadiye, root cause (asli wajah) dhoondhiye aur control likhiye (agli baar ise kya rokega).',
  'Do not fix anything straight away. First, save the failure as it happened, like a photo of the scene.':
    'Turant kuch fix mat kijiye. Pehle failure ko waise hi save kijiye jaise woh hui, jaise ghatna ki photo.',
  'A security and red-team report (attacks you tried on your own system), plus a table of failure findings.':
    'Ek security aur red-team report (aapne apne hi system par jo attacks try kiye), saath mein failure findings ki ek table.',
  'Which three failures surprised you?':
    'Kaun si teen failures ne aapko hairan kiya?',
  'Your failure log is often worth more than your happy-path demo (the demo where nothing goes wrong).':
    'Aapka failure log aksar aapke happy-path demo se zyada keemti hota hai (woh demo jisme kuch galat nahi hota).',
  'Observe: prove it is inspectable before real users touch it':
    'Observe: saabit kijiye ki ise andar se dekha ja sakta hai, real users ke chhoone se pehle',
  'Prove that you can see inside the system, and that its risk has limits, before you ship it to a real user.':
    'Saabit kijiye ki aap system ke andar dekh sakte hain, aur uske risk ki seema hai, real user ko ship karne se pehle.',
  'If you cannot observe a system, you are guessing about it, however good the demo looks. It is like driving with the dashboard covered.':
    'Agar aap system ko observe nahi kar sakte, toh aap andaza laga rahe hain, demo kitna bhi achha lage. Yeh dashboard dhak kar gaadi chalane jaisa hai.',
  'Run the security release-gate checks and the observability thresholds (the lines you must not cross) against the current build.':
    'Security release-gate checks aur observability thresholds (woh lakeeren jo paar nahi karni) current build par chalaiye.',
  'Connect a six-panel dashboard to the real traces from your evaluation and break stages. The panels are quality, safety, latency, cost, traffic and failures.':
    'Chhe panels wala dashboard apne evaluation aur break stages ke real traces se jodiye. Panels hain quality, safety, latency, cost, traffic aur failures.',
  'Take one signal off the dashboard, and see which incident (a problem that happens) becomes invisible without it.':
    'Dashboard se ek signal hata dijiye, aur dekhiye ki uske bina kaun si incident (jo problem hoti hai) dikhna band ho jati hai.',
  'A security go/no-go checklist and an observability dashboard.':
    'Ek security go/no-go checklist aur ek observability dashboard.',
  'Which alert should wake a human, and which should only be written in the log?':
    'Kaun sa alert insaan ko jagaye, aur kaun sa sirf log mein likha jaye?',
  'A stakeholder will ask, "how would you know if this broke at 2am?" This stage is your answer.':
    'Ek stakeholder poochhega, "how would you know if this broke at 2am?" Yeh stage aapka jawaab hai.',
  'Ship to real users':
    'Ship to real users',
  'Launch to a real group of users, and prove that the rollback (the undo button) really works.':
    'Real users ke group ko launch kijiye, aur saabit kijiye ki rollback (undo button) sach mein kaam karta hai.',
  'Real users teach you more than more theory. Five to ten watched sessions beat another week of polishing on your own.':
    'Real users zyada theory se zyada sikhate hain. Paanch se das dekhe gaye sessions, akele ek aur hafte ki chamak-damak se behtar hain.',
  'Put it on a stable live URL with monitoring, analytics (counting how people use it), a privacy notice, a way for users to send feedback, and a rollback procedure.':
    'Ise ek stable live URL par rakhiye: monitoring, analytics (logon ke use ki ginti), privacy notice, users ke feedback bhejne ka tareeka, aur rollback procedure ke saath.',
  'Recruit five to ten real target users. Watch at least five sessions, and keep a support log from day one.':
    'Paanch se das real target users bulaiye. Kam se kam paanch sessions dekhiye, aur pehle din se support log rakhiye.',
  'Test your rollback for real before launch. Release a harmless change, undo it, and time it. A rollback you have never tried is only a plan.':
    'Launch se pehle apna rollback sach mein test kijiye. Ek harmless badlav release kijiye, use wapas lijiye, aur samay naapiye. Jo rollback kabhi try nahi kiya, woh sirf ek plan hai.',
  'A live URL, a support log, a funnel or cohort view (how users move through, or groups of users over time), and a timed rollback test.':
    'Ek live URL, ek support log, ek funnel ya cohort view (users kaise aage badhte hain, ya samay ke saath users ke group), aur ek timed rollback test.',
  'Do you have evidence from real users, not just demo traffic from your teammates?':
    'Kya aapke paas real users ka evidence hai, sirf aapke teammates ka demo traffic nahi?',
  'A system that is beautifully evaluated but never used by anyone has not passed this stage yet.':
    'Jo system bahut achhe se evaluate hua hai par kisi ne use nahi kiya, woh abhi is stage se paas nahi hua.',
  'Measure: optimize and quantify unit economics':
    'Measure: optimize and quantify unit economics',
  'Apply targeted fixes, measure before and after, and show that the money works at scale (when many people use it).':
    'Nishane par fixes lagaiye, pehle aur baad naapiye, aur dikhaiye ki paisa scale par kaam karta hai (jab bahut log use karein).',
  'An optimisation (making something better) means something only when you can see the difference it made. And the idea is viable (it can survive) only when you can also see the cost of each successful task.':
    'Optimisation (kisi cheez ko behtar banana) tabhi maayne rakhta hai jab aap uska farak dekh sakein. Aur idea viable hai (tik sakta hai) tabhi jab aap har safal task ki keemat bhi dekh sakein.',
  'Try reranking (re-sorting the found pages), restructuring the prompt, validating against a schema, caching (keeping answers to reuse), routing or trimming the context, wherever it makes sense. Then work out the model cost per successful task at low, base and high usage.':
    'Reranking (mile hue pages ko dobara sajana), prompt ko dobara banana, schema se validate karna, caching (jawaab dobara kaam aane ke liye rakhna), routing ya context ko chhota karna, jahan bhi theek baithe, try kijiye. Phir low, base aur high usage par har safal task ka model cost nikaliye.',
  'Change one thing at a time, and rerun the part of the evaluation that matters for it.':
    'Ek baar mein ek cheez badliye, aur evaluation ka woh hissa dobara chalaiye jo usse jude.',
  'Keep a fix that improves one metric but makes another worse, and write down the trade-off (what you gave up). Then check what happens to your margin (profit per task) at ten times the volume.':
    'Ek aisa fix rakhiye jo ek metric behtar kare par doosra bigade, aur trade-off likhiye (aapne kya chhoda). Phir dekhiye ki dus guna volume par aapka margin (har task ka munafa) kya hota hai.',
  'A before-and-after optimisation report, ADRs and a unit-economics model.':
    'Pehle aur baad ki optimisation report, ADRs aur ek unit-economics model.',
  'Which improvement is big enough to matter, in the numbers or in real life? Does revenue (money coming in) grow faster than the cost of running the model and supporting users?':
    'Kaun sa improvement itna bada hai ki fark pade, numbers mein ya asli zindagi mein? Kya revenue (aane wala paisa) model chalane aur users ko support karne ki cost se tezi se badhta hai?',
  'Avoid "optimization theater" (looking busy without real gains). Also avoid a model that works only at your current, subsidised scale (a price someone else is paying for you).':
    '"Optimization theater" se bachiye (kaam dikhana par asli fayda nahi). Aisa model bhi mat rakhiye jo sirf aapke abhi ke subsidised scale par chalta ho (jiska daam koi aur aapke liye de raha ho).',
  'Iterate from evidence':
    'Iterate from evidence',
  'Ship one improvement that is based on evidence, and log the decision behind it.':
    'Ek improvement ship kijiye jo evidence par based ho, aur uske peeche ka faisla log kijiye.',
  'An iteration (one more round of improving) earns its place only when it is tied to a problem you measured, not a hunch.':
    'Iteration (sudhaar ka ek aur daur) tabhi apni jagah paata hai jab woh aapki naapi hui problem se judi ho, andaze se nahi.',
  'Pick the lowest-scoring evidence question from the earlier stages, and ship one change aimed at it.':
    'Pichhle stages ka sabse kam number wala evidence question chuniye, aur usi par nishana lagakar ek badlav ship kijiye.',
  'Compare the product and AI metrics before and after. Then write the decision into the decision log: the context, the options you considered, the approach you chose, the trade-offs, and what would make you reopen it.':
    'Product aur AI metrics pehle aur baad ke milaiye. Phir faisla decision log mein likhiye: context, jo options aapne socha, jo tareeka chuna, trade-offs, aur kya hone par aap ise dobara kholenge.',
  'Ask someone to push back on the iteration you shipped, and answer with the metric, not with opinion.':
    'Kisi se kahiye ki aapke ship kiye iteration par sawaal uthaye, aur metric se jawaab dijiye, raay se nahi.',
  'A changelog (a list of what changed) and one filled-in decision-log entry.':
    'Ek changelog (kya badla uski list) aur decision-log ki ek bhari hui entry.',
  'Can you tie the iteration to a problem you measured, not a preference?':
    'Kya aap iteration ko ek naapi hui problem se jod sakte hain, pasand se nahi?',
  'If the decision log is still empty at this stage, then every choice so far went unwritten. That is the first thing a reviewer will ask about.':
    'Agar is stage par bhi decision log khaali hai, toh ab tak ka har faisla likha nahi gaya. Reviewer sabse pehle yahi poochhega.',
  'Defend the system':
    'Defend the system',
  'Present the architecture, the evidence, the security, the cost, the viability (can it survive) and the risk that remains, to two different audiences.':
    'Architecture, evidence, security, cost, viability (kya yeh tik sakta hai) aur bacha hua risk, do alag shrotaon ke saamne rakhiye.',
  'The last skill this course tests is explaining and defending the system you built.':
    'Is course ka aakhri hunar jo parakha jata hai, woh hai aapke banaye system ko samjhana aur uska bachav karna.',
  'Prepare a five-minute briefing for executives and a twenty-minute technical defence.':
    'Executives ke liye paanch minute ki briefing aur bees minute ka technical defence taiyar kijiye.',
  'Build a ten-slide deck and a public case study. Cover the problem and the evidence that it is real. Then say what you built, whether it works, whether it is safe, whether people use it, and whether it makes money. Finish with what failed, the trade-offs, and what you are asking for next.':
    'Das slides ka deck aur ek public case study banaiye. Problem aur uske sachche hone ka evidence batayiye. Phir batayiye aapne kya banaya, kya yeh kaam karta hai, kya yeh safe hai, kya log ise use karte hain, aur kya yeh paisa kamata hai. Ant mein batayiye kya fail hua, trade-offs, aur aap aage kya maang rahe hain.',
  'Ask a peer to challenge your model choice, your agent choice, your security and your cost assumptions.':
    'Kisi saathi se kahiye ki woh aapke model choice, agent choice, security aur cost assumptions ko chunauti de.',
  'The final repository, architecture, PRD, risk register, evaluation pack, observability dashboard, vendor scorecard, decision log, executive deck and public case study.':
    'Aakhri repository, architecture, PRD, risk register, evaluation pack, observability dashboard, vendor scorecard, decision log, executive deck aur public case study.',
  'Can you explain every major decision without saying "the framework handles it"?':
    'Kya aap har bade faisle ko bina "the framework handles it" kahe samjha sakte hain?',
  'This is the credential (the proof of skill) you carry into interviews and design reviews.':
    'Yeh woh credential hai (hunar ka saboot) jo aap interviews aur design reviews mein le jaate hain.',
  'When you are done':
    'When you are done',
  'How do you know you are finished? Check this list, like a packing list before a trip. You are done when every line is true.':
    'Aapko kaise pata chale ki aap khatam kar chuke hain? Yeh list jaanchiye, jaise safar se pehle packing list. Aap tab khatam hain jab har line sach ho.',
  'It runs on a machine that is not yours, at a live URL.':
    'Yeh aise machine par chalta hai jo aapki nahi hai, ek live URL par.',
  'Anyone can rerun the tests and get the same numbers.':
    'Koi bhi tests dobara chala kar wahi numbers pa sakta hai.',
  'You have broken it in at least three real ways, on purpose.':
    'Aapne ise kam se kam teen asli tareekon se, jaan-boojhkar toda hai.',
  'Three of your fixes have before and after numbers.':
    'Aapke teen fixes ke pehle aur baad ke numbers hain.',
  'You attacked your own safeguards (the things meant to protect it) and wrote down what happened.':
    'Aapne apne hi safeguards par hamla kiya (jo cheezein ise bachane ke liye hain) aur likha ki kya hua.',
  'You know how to roll it back (undo it) if something goes wrong, and you have timed it.':
    'Aap jaante hain ki kuch galat ho toh ise rollback kaise karna hai (wapas lena), aur aapne samay naapa hai.',
  'Five to ten real users have used it, and you have their evidence, not a teammate\'s.':
    'Paanch se das real users ne ise use kiya hai, aur aapke paas unka evidence hai, kisi teammate ka nahi.',
  'You can state cost per successful task, and what happens to margin (profit per task) at ten times the volume.':
    'Aap cost per successful task bata sakte hain, aur dus guna volume par margin (har task ka munafa) ka kya hota hai.',
  'At least one iteration is tied to measured evidence, and written in the decision log.':
    'Kam se kam ek iteration naape hue evidence se judi hai, aur decision log mein likhi hai.',
  'You can explain every major choice without saying "the framework handles that".':
    'Aap har bade choice ko bina "the framework handles that" kahe samjha sakte hain.',
  'Final self-test':
    'Final self-test',
  'One last exercise, like a quiz you give yourself. Answer these from memory, out loud if you can. If you get stuck on one, go back to the chapter that covers it.':
    'Ek aakhri abhyaas, jaise khud ko diya gaya quiz. Inke jawaab yaad se dijiye, ho sake toh zor se bol kar. Agar kisi par atak jaayen, toh us chapter par wapas jaiye jo use cover karta hai.',
  'Why is the model stateless (like a goldfish that forgets)?':
    'Model stateless kyun hai (jaise woh goldfish jo bhool jati hai)?',
  'Why can retrieval fail, and why do embeddings (an address for meaning) help?':
    'Retrieval fail kyun ho sakta hai, aur embeddings (matlab ka pata) madad kyun karte hain?',
  'Why does retrieval never say "nothing here" on its own?':
    'Retrieval apne aap kabhi "nothing here" kyun nahi kehta?',
  'Why is top-k (how many pages you pick) a trade-off?':
    'Top-k (aap kitne pages chunte hain) trade-off kyun hai?',
  'When does a workflow beat an agent?':
    'Workflow agent se kab behtar hota hai?',
  'Why are tools a security boundary (a fence)?':
    'Tools security boundary (ek baad) kyun hain?',
  'Why must evaluation come before release, and why does tracing matter?':
    'Evaluation release se pehle kyun aana chahiye, aur tracing kyun zaroori hai?',
  'How do you work out cost per successful task, not per call?':
    'Aap cost per successful task kaise nikalte hain, per call nahi?',
  'What makes an AI specification (the spec, a recipe card) different from a normal one?':
    'Ek AI specification (spec, ek recipe card) normal wale se alag kyun hoti hai?',
  'Who is your buyer, and does your price cover what the model costs you?':
    'Aapka buyer kaun hai, aur kya aapka price woh cover karta hai jo model aapko cost karta hai?',
  'What would make you kill this project rather than keep iterating on it?':
    'Kya hone par aap is project ko kill kar denge, bajaye iterate karte rehne ke?',
  'If you can answer all of these, you have done what the course set out to do. You can build an AI system, break it, measure it, and stand behind it.':
    'Agar aap in sabke jawaab de sakte hain, toh aapne woh kar liya jo course karna chahta tha. Aap ek AI system bana sakte hain, use tod sakte hain, measure kar sakte hain, aur uske peeche khade ho sakte hain.',
  'This was the last required chapter. Next, Track B begins with B1, on business models, pricing and unit economics. Later, B7 turns what you just built into stories for interviews. The appendices turn your artifacts (the papers you made) into reusable portfolio templates.':
    'Yeh aakhri required chapter tha. Aage Track B shuru hota hai B1 se, jo business models, pricing aur unit economics ke baare mein hai. Baad mein B7 aapne jo abhi banaya use interviews ki kahaniyon mein badalta hai. Appendices aapke artifacts (aapke banaye papers) ko reusable portfolio templates mein badalte hain.',
});
